@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Inputs}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Accessibility}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Inputs @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Inputs @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</h1>
<p>
    All BlazorRamp input components share a consistent accessibility foundation, ensuring a predictable and inclusive experience across your forms regardless of which input type you use
</p>

<h2>Structure</h2>
<p>
    Every input is provided as a self-contained unit comprising a label, an optional hint text area, the input field itself, and a validation message area. This consistent structure means
    that regardless of the input type, screen reader users always encounter information in a logical, predictable order.
</p>

<h2>Validation announcements</h2>
<p>
    None of the BlazorRamp inputs use ARIA live regions for validation feedback. Whilst live regions may seem an obvious choice, they can introduce confusion and verbosity in practice. When using
    <span class="code-span">oninput</span> for immediate validation, live region announcements would fire on every keystroke as the field transitions between valid and invalid states, which quickly becomes disruptive. When using
    <span class="code-span">onchange</span>, a screen reader user moving to the next field may hear the name of their newly focused field immediately followed by a validation announcement relating to the field they just left, which
    can be disorienting.
</p>
<p>
    Instead, validation messages are surfaced via aria-describedby and the <span class="code-span">ValidationDisplayMode</span> parameter, giving you precise control over how and when error information is presented.
</p>

<h2>Required fields</h2>
<p>
    By default all components have the <span class="code-span">Required</span> parameter set to true. This adds a red asterisk next to the field label which is not part of the accessible name and is hidden from screen readers.
    The attribute <span class="code-span">aria-required</span> is then added to the input for screen readers. Alternatively you can set the <span class="code-span">Required</span> to false, and add the text "Required" to the hint 
    or label text for both sighted or screen reader users. Please note if you add this to the <span class="code-span">LabelText</span> parameter it will become part of the accessible name.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>If taking the asterisk approach don't forget to add a note at the top of the form informing sighted users that the asterisk denotes a required field.
</p>

<h2>Hint text and validation messages</h2>
<p>
    When hint text is provided, it is automatically associated with the input via <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span>. When validation errors are present, retrieved via the Blazor <span class="code-span">EditContext</span>,
    the error area's id is also added to the <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> attribute. When a user focuses the input, the screen reader announces the content of all associated elements in the order they appear - label first,
    then hint text, then any validation errors.
</p>


<h2>ValidationDisplayMode options</h2>
<p>
    The <span class="code-span">ValidationDisplayMode</span> parameter gives you three options for how hint text and validation messages are presented to screen reader users.
</p>


<h3>DescribedByWithHint</h3>
<p>
    This the default mode. Both the hint text and any validation errors are associated via <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> and announced when the input receives focus. This is appropriate when
    both the hint and the error message together provide the most complete guidance.
</p>

<h3>DescribedByHintSuppressed</h3>
<p>
    This removes the hint text from <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> when validation errors are present. This reduces verbosity for screen reader users in cases where the error message alone contains
    sufficient information to correct the problem, avoiding the repetition of hearing the hint text on every focus.
</p>

<h3>TabbableWithHint</h3>
<p>
    This takes a different approach entirely. Rather than relying on the user refocusing the input to hear validation errors, this mode renders the error area as a focusable landmark region with a tab stop. When the user tabs
    away from an invalid field, their next stop is the error area itself, where they can read the error information directly. All error areas also appear in the user's landmark list, making it straightforward to review and
    navigate between all errors on the page.
</p>


<h2>Recommended approach</h2>
<p>
    My preferred configuration is to use <span class="code-span">UpdateOnInput="true"</span> combined with <span class="hint">ValidationDisplayMode="TabbableWithHint"</span>. This gives sighted users immediate visual feedback
    as they type, whilst ensuring screen reader users land on the error area when they tab away from an invalid field - providing timely, contextual feedback without any interruptions whilst typing.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>Some screen reader browser combinations especially on Chromium browsers treat <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> like a live region so with the <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> option
    when the field changes from valid to invalid the error message will be announced. 
</p>    
<p>
    <strong>Important: </strong>For WCAG dependant on the context, you should ensure that the inputs have the appropriate autocomplete values specified. 
    You can add any of these to the Input component directly as attribute splatting will place these on the underlying input element.
</p>

<h2 id="device-testing">Accessibility device testing</h2>

<ul aria-labelledby="device-testing">
    <li>JAWS, Narrator and NVDA on Windows were each paired with every one of the following browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on macOS was paired with Apple's Safari.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on iPhone was paired with mobile Safari.</li>
    <li>Talkback on Android was paired with mobile Google Chrome.</li>
    <li>Voice access on Windows 11</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> Testing was performed with the latest browser and assistive technologies versions that were available at the time of testing (see release history on the installation page for approximate testing dates)
</p>
<h2 id="screen-reader-quirks" tabindex="-1">Screen reader quirks or known issues</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="screen-reader-quirks">
    <li>
        <b>Narrator</b> on Firefox currently does not seem to honour <span class="code-span">aria-required</span> or  <span class="code-span">aria-invalid</span>; please check your version
        of Narrator and version of Firefox if you need to support this pairing. Its recommended that Narrator be used with Edge or Chrome. By default the input components Required parameter is set to true which
        adds <span class="code-span">aria-required</span> to the input. An alternative, is to set the Required parameter to false and add the text "Required" at the start of the hint text.
    </li>

    <li>
        <h3>Radio input group quirks</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Error Announcement on Selection - When a radio option is selected and validation runs, screen readers will not immediately announce the error message linked via aria-describedby. This is expected behaviour and is not a defect.
                Because keyboard navigation between radio buttons within a role="radiogroup" uses the arrow keys, focus never leaves and re-enters the group element itself. Screen readers only re-read a group's
                <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> association on group entry — since focus remains within the group when an option is selected and validation fires, the updated description is not
                announced until the user tabs away and returns to the group. <strong>Using the tabbable region option may be preferable.</strong>
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Narrator</b> on all browsers does not honour <span class="code-span">aria-invalid</span> nor does it announce hint text and error messages linked via <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <h3>Time & Date input group quirks</h3>
        Both the time & date inputs are composite components that make use of the ARIA role attribute of "group" given there are no named roles applicable to these inputs. Given some of the issues I encountered using screen readers (especially repeated announcements) when 
        wrapping a native <span class="code-span">input type="time"</span> and <span class="code-span">input type="date"</span> element, this approach seemed preferable despite its issues.
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Narrator</b> on all browsers does not announce the hint text or error messages that are both linked via <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span>. It also does not announce aria-invalid
                that is placed on the group. It was a deliberate decision not to place <span class="code-span">aria-invalid</span> on individual input segments as hearing invalid on a segment that may be valid,
                due to another segment and/or the overall time being invalid due to a validation rule failure may have been too confusing.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>NVDA</b> on all browsers does not treat <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> as a live region for composite inputs as it does for standard inputs. When an error message ID is dynamically added to <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span>,
                the message is not announced immediately — it is only read when the user returns focus to the component.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>JAWS</b> on all browsers announces the group label and anything linked via <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> twice on entering the component. This is consistent with how JAWS handles <span class="code-span">role="group"</span> and is not a defect.
                    Like NVDA, JAWS does not treat <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> as a live region for composite inputs — dynamically added error messages are not announced immediately and are only read when the user returns focus to the component.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>VoiceOver</b> on macOS does not treat <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> as a live region for composite inputs as it does for standard inputs. When an error message ID is dynamically added to <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span>,
                the message is not announced immediately - it is only read when the user returns focus to the component.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>VoiceOver</b> on iOS reads <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> on focus entry to each individual segment within the group. This means an error caused by one segment will be re-announced when focus moves to subsequent segments, which may result in 
                repeated error announcements across the hours, minutes, and seconds inputs.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>TalkBack</b> on Android when using touch exploration (pressing directly on a segment) announces only the current value of the input without reading the accessible name. When a segment is empty, as with a nullable TimeOnly? or DateOnly? field, the accessible name is announced correctly.
                This is consistent with TalkBack's native behaviour for numeric inputs and is not a defect. Users navigating by linear swipe will have the label and value read correctly in sequence.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>
<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>Not a quirk as its by design, but Talkback on Android will not read content linked by the <span class="code-span">aria-describedby</span> attribute on the input when the input has content.
</p>
<p>
I recommend prioritising form-level validation over field-level validation. Ensure a robust error summary mechanism is in place at the form level first, then layer field-level validation on top. When the user 
    attempts to submit a form with errors, use an ARIA live region (preferably the Live Region Service) to announce that errors are present and direct the user to the error summary region to review them.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong>If using the Blazor Ramp Inputs you can also use the <span class="code-span">&lt;InputErrorsSummary /&gt;</span> that on form submission, if errors are present, will be displayed with links to the erroneous fields. No live regions announcements
    are needed given the focusing and <span class="code-span">aria-labelledby</span> attribute used.
</p>
